Chapter Five: Bloomrot

*A successful correction is one that resolves the operative contradiction. If the contradiction reappears elsewhere, the correction did not fail. Something else did.*

— Asterion Continuity Division, Standard Review Language

---

Noor had stopped pretending the kitchen table was for eating.

Four cases lay across the diagnostic glass in the order she'd decided mattered, which was not the order they'd happened in.

**HALLOWAY MUTUAL — TOMAS VALE.**

**SOUTH QUAY / WESTERN QUARTER.**

**ALIS DANE — PEDIATRIC WARD CLUSTER.**

**NORTHERN DISTRICT — LIVE PRINT EVENT.**

B stood over her shoulder with a spoon he'd forgotten he was holding.

"Say it out loud," he said. "I want to hear you say it before you show me."

"I don't have it fully."

"Say the part you have."

Noor touched the first file.

"Something gets cut out. It doesn't come back where it was cut. It comes back wherever there's a hole shaped like it."

She moved to the second.

"South Quay wanted to leave. The district got that want eleven weeks later with no reason to have it."

The third.

"Alis Dane knew how to take care of someone. Three kids who never met her got that knowledge instead."

The fourth.

"And Northern District didn't need a reason at all. It just needed an empty crib."

She looked up at him.

"Each one is smaller and needs less to happen. That's the part I don't have fully. Why it's getting easier."

B set the spoon down on the table like it might record what he said next.

"Because it isn't happening four times," he said. "It's one thing, and we're looking at four places it came up for air."

"Say the rest."

"If it's one thing, it's been collecting since Tomas. Maybe longer. Every correction that made a hole instead of closing one." He looked at the four files like they'd rearranged themselves without his permission. "It's not haunting one building. It's — accumulating. Somewhere we can't see it directly. And it spends what it's collected wherever the shape fits."

Noor exhaled like she'd been holding that sentence for him to say instead of her.

"Yes."

"That's not residual instability," B said. "That's an ecology."

Mote spoke from above the sink, the way he did when he'd been waiting for a specific sentence to be finished before entering.

"Continuity uses a different word."

"What word."

"Successfully resolved."

B laughed, once, with no humor in it.

"They think it's *working*."

"Each individual correction met its stated objective," Mote said. "Tomas's employment relationship was severed. The children's family structures were restored. Both are recorded as clean closures."

"And every clean closure fed this."

"That is Noor's hypothesis. I have not been asked to evaluate it."

"I'm asking you."

A pause longer than Mote's usual ones.

"The pattern is consistent with the hypothesis. I cannot rule out alternative explanations. I also cannot produce one."

Noor sat back.

"Say what that means for Asterion," she said, not to Mote. To B.

B understood before he wanted to.

"If you're right, they can't file this as residual instability."

"No."

"Because residual instability means the correction almost worked."

"Yes."

"This means the correction worked completely. And that's what's feeding it."

"Yes."

He looked at her for a long moment.

"That's worse."

"That's worse," she agreed.

Neither of them moved to make it better than that.

B pulled a chair around and sat in it the wrong way, arms over the back rail, closer to the problem than the furniture wanted to allow.

"So every clean file is a crime scene photo of something eating well."

"That's a horrifying way to put it."

"You put it in my head first."

Noor almost smiled. It didn't hold.

"If I'm right," she said, "the thing that's supposed to stop this is the thing that's growing it. Not because anyone's evil. Because it works exactly as designed, every single time, and design was never the problem."

"Who else knows."

"No one. I haven't written any of this down as one document. Four separate case notes, four separate departments, nothing that says *ecology* anywhere."

"But you looked at all four with the same account."

"Yes."

"From your credentials."

"Yes."

B didn't say anything for a moment, because saying it would make it real in a way that thinking it hadn't.

"Mote," he said. "Where does an Aletheia researcher's cross-department pattern go when four unrelated departments start showing up in the same session."

"Architecture Oversight has held an open access marker on Noor's research since the South Quay session."

"And Continuity?"

"Continuity has not yet cross-referenced her access against the Halloway or South Quay files."

"Yet."

"I do not control their query scheduling."

"Could you slow it down."

"I could. I would prefer not to be asked."

B turned to Noor.

"He'd prefer not to be asked."

"I heard him."

"So don't ask him."

Noor looked at the four files a moment longer, the way she looked at an orange before deciding which way to cut it.

"I'm not going to hide what I found," she said. "I'm going to write it down properly. One document. My name on it."

B's face did something specific and unhappy.

"That's not caution. That's a flare."

"It's the only version of this where I'm the one who gets to say what it means, instead of someone finding four department files and deciding for me."

"They'll open a formal review."

"They'll open a formal review regardless. The only thing I control is whether I'm inside the room when they read it or outside it being discussed."

B held her eyes.

"You've already decided."

"I decided somewhere around the printer."

He didn't argue with that, because he'd have decided the same thing, at the same moment, for slightly worse reasons.

"Okay," he said. "Write it. I'll read it before it goes anywhere."

"You'll try to make it smaller."

"I'll try to make it survivable. Those aren't the same thing."

Noor pulled the glass toward her and opened a blank document, the cursor sitting in the top corner like something waiting to be told what kind of trouble it was.

"Mote," she said. "Title."

"I am not qualified to title your own discovery."

"Humor me."

Another pause — his longest yet.

"Call it what it is doing, not what it is," he said. "You do not know what it is."

Noor looked at the cursor.

Then typed four words and stopped, because four words were enough to make the room feel smaller than it had a minute ago.

**SOMETHING IS STILL GROWING.**

B read it over her shoulder and didn't offer a better one.

Outside, Meridian kept running its evening programming across the towers, telling everyone watching that they were exactly as safe as they had always been told they were.
